            \"output\": \"<path>/Users/ueharal/Documents/ChatGPT/canvas2/assignments/CS5351_TradingAgents_safety_gate_trial/tradingagents/agents/schemas.py</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: \\"\\"\\"Pydantic schemas used by agents that produce structured output.\n2: \n3: The framework's primary artifact is still prose: each agent's natural-language\n4: reasoning is what users read in the saved markdown reports and what the\n5: downstream agents read as context.  Structured output is layered onto the\n6: three decision-making agents (Research Manager, Trader, Portfolio Manager)\n7: so that:\n8: \n9: - Their outputs follow consistent section headers across runs and providers\n10: - Each provider's native structured-output mode is used (json_schema for\n11:   OpenAI/xAI, response_schema for Gemini, tool-use for Anthropic)\n12: - Schema field descriptions become the model's output instructions, freeing\n13:   the prompt body to focus on context and the rating-scale guidance\n14: - A render helper turns the parsed Pydantic instance back into the same\n15:   markdown shape the rest of the system already consumes, so display,\n16:   memory log, and saved reports keep working unchanged\n17: \\"\\"\\"\n18: \n19: from __future__ import annotations\n20: \n21: from enum import StrEnum\n22: from typing import Literal\n23: \n24: from pydantic import BaseModel, Field, field_validator\n25: \n26: # LLMs sometimes write a placeholder string (\\"None\\", \\"N/A\\", ...) into an optional\n27: # numeric field instead of omitting it. Coerce those to None so the structured\n28: # call validates instead of erroring (#1058). Pydantic still parses real numeric\n29: # strings (\\"189.5\\") to float.\n30: _NULLISH_FLOAT = {\\"\\", \\"none\\", \\"n/a\\", \\"na\\", \\"null\\", \\"nil\\", \\"-\\", \\"tbd\\", \\"unknown\\"}\n31: \n32: \n33: def _coerce_optional_float(value):\n34:     \\"\\"\\"Normalise an LLM-written optional numeric field before validation.\n35: \n36:     Three shapes show up in practice: a placeholder string (\\"None\\", \\"N/A\\") in\n37:     place of an omitted value (#1058); a percentage where a price was asked for\n38:     (\\"15%\\", #1288); and a human-formatted price (\\"$1,234.50\\"). A percentage\n39:     cannot be salvaged into an absolute level -- reading \\"15%\\" as 15 would put a\n40:     stop at $15 on a $600 stock -- so it is dropped like a placeholder, leaving\n41:     one bad field to null out instead of failing the whole proposal. A formatted\n42:     price is reduced to its number.\n43: \n44:     Anything that is not a single number is dropped the same way. A range\n45:     (\\"150-160\\") or a hedge (\\"around 150\\") would otherwise reach pydantic, fail\n46:     validation, and discard the whole decision, losing every field the model got\n47:     right along with the price.\n48:     \\"\\"\\"\n49:     if not isinstance(value, str):\n50:         return value\n51:     text = value.strip()\n52:     if text.lower() in _NULLISH_FLOAT or text.endswith(\\"%\\"):\n53:         return None\n54:     cleaned = text.replace(\\",\\", \\"\\").lstrip(\\"$€£¥\\").strip()\n55:     try:\n56:         return float(cleaned)\n57:     except ValueError:\n58:         return None\n59: \n60: \n61: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n62: # Shared rating types\n63: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n64: \n65: \n66: class PortfolioRating(StrEnum):\n67:     \\"\\"\\"5-tier rating used by the Research Manager and Portfolio Manager.\\"\\"\\"\n68: \n69:     BUY = \\"Buy\\"\n70:     OVERWEIGHT = \\"Overweight\\"\n71:     HOLD = \\"Hold\\"\n72:     UNDERWEIGHT = \\"Underweight\\"\n73:     SELL = \\"Sell\\"\n74: \n75: \n76: class TraderAction(StrEnum):\n77:     \\"\\"\\"3-tier transaction direction used by the Trader.\n78: \n79:     The Trader's job is to translate the Research Manager's investment plan\n80:     into a concrete transaction proposal: should the desk execute a Buy, a\n81:     Sell, or sit on Hold this round.  Position sizing and the nuanced\n82:     Overweight / Underweight calls happen later at the Portfolio Manager.\n83:     \\"\\"\\"\n84: \n85:     BUY = \\"Buy\\"\n86:     HOLD = \\"Hold\\"\n87:     SELL = \\"Sell\\"\n88: \n89: \n90: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n91: # Research Manager\n92: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n93: \n94: \n95: class ResearchPlan(BaseModel):\n96:     \\"\\"\\"Structured investment plan produced by the Research Manager.\n97: \n98:     Hand-off to the Trader: the recommendation pins the directional view,\n99:     the rationale captures which side of the bull/bear debate carried the\n100:     argument, and the strategic actions translate that into concrete\n101:     instructions the trader can execute against.\n102:     \\"\\"\\"\n103: \n104:     recommendation: PortfolioRating = Field(\n105:         description=(\n106:             \\"The investment recommendation. Exactly one of Buy / Overweight / \\"\n107:             \\"Hold / Underweight / Sell. Conflicting arguments alone are not a \\"\n108:             \\"reason to Hold: commit to the stronger side, sized by how \\"\n109:             \\"decisively it wins. Choose Hold only when the evidence is still \\"\n110:             \\"balanced after weighing, or too thin to support a call.\\"\n111:         ),\n112:     )\n113:     rationale: str = Field(\n114:         description=(\n115:             \\"Conversational summary of the key points from both sides of the \\"\n116:             \\"debate, ending with which arguments led to the recommendation. \\"\n117:             \\"Speak naturally, as if to a teammate.\\"\n118:         ),\n119:     )\n120:     strategic_actions: str = Field(\n121:         description=(\n122:             \\"Concrete steps for the trader to implement the recommendation, \\"\n123:             \\"including sizing guidance relative to a standard allocation. The \\"\n124:             \\"research team does not see the caller's holdings; the trader and \\"\n125:             \\"portfolio manager apply the actual position.\\"\n126:         ),\n127:     )\n128: \n129: \n130: def render_research_plan(plan: ResearchPlan) -> str:\n131:     \\"\\"\\"Render a ResearchPlan to markdown for storage and the trader's prompt context.\\"\\"\\"\n132:     return \\"\\n\\".join([\n133:         f\\"**Recommendation**: {plan.recommendation.value}\\",\n134:         \\"\\",\n135:         f\\"**Rationale**: {plan.rationale}\\",\n136:         \\"\\",\n137:         f\\"**Strategic Actions**: {plan.strategic_actions}\\",\n138:     ])\n139: \n140: \n141: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n142: # Trader\n143: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n144: \n145: \n146: class TraderProposal(BaseModel):\n147:     \\"\\"\\"Structured transaction proposal produced by the Trader.\n148: \n149:     The trader reads the Research Manager's investment plan and the analyst\n150:     reports, then turns them into a concrete transaction: what action to\n151:     take, the reasoning that justifies it, and the practical levels for\n152:     entry, stop-loss, and sizing.\n153:     \\"\\"\\"\n154: \n155:     action: TraderAction = Field(\n156:         description=\\"The transaction direction. Exactly one of Buy / Hold / Sell.\\",\n157:     )\n158:     reasoning: str = Field(\n159:         description=(\n160:             \\"The case for this action, anchored in the analysts' reports and \\"\n161:             \\"the research plan. Two to four sentences.\\"\n162:         ),\n163:     )\n164:     entry_price: float | None = Field(\n165:         default=None,\n166:         description=(\n167:             \\"Optional entry price target as an absolute number in the instrument's \\"\n168:             \\"quote currency (e.g. 189.5), never a percentage or a 